For a retraction of a continuous lattice, the Scott topology of the retract coincides with the subspace topology induced from the ambient lattice along the inclusion. This is Scott's Proposition 2.10(b), including the March 1972 correction.

An induced-open set is the preimage of a Scott-open set of the ambient lattice, and that preimage is Scott-open because the inclusion preserves directed suprema. Conversely, if a Scott-open set U of the retract contains d, the supremum of the retractions of the ambient elements way below the inclusion of d equals d and therefore lies in U. That family is nonempty and directed, so some such retraction lies in U. The preimage of the set of ambient elements way above that witness is then a neighborhood of d contained in U, because the retraction of the witness lies below any such point and U is an upper set. The preimage is induced-open, being the preimage of a Scott-open set.
